<!--
  RULE: vuln-analysis-owasp
  Analiza el código para identificar vulnerabilidades según OWASP Top 10 y sugiere refactorings.
-->
Analyze the following code for potential security vulnerabilities, focusing on OWASP Top 10 (e.g., Injection, XSS, Broken Auth, Security Misconfiguration). For each issue found, suggest a mitigation with referencia a OWASP Top 10.

<!--
  RULE: no-hardcoded-credentials
  Detecta y elimina credenciales y datos sensibles en claro.
-->
Scan the code for hardcoded credentials, API keys, tokens, or sensitive information. Recommend moving secrets to secure storage (environment variables, vault) and show example code changes.

<!--
  RULE: input-validation
  Asegura validación de entrada rigurosa.
-->
Review input handling and identify missing or insufficient validation. Propose robust validation logic for strings, numbers, dates, and file uploads to prevent injection attacks.

<!--
  RULE: sql-injection
  Protege contra inyección SQL.
-->
Identify SQL queries built via string concatenation or interpolation. Refactor to use parameterized queries or ORM best practices explaining how this prevents SQL injection.

<!--
  RULE: xss-protection
  Evita Cross-Site Scripting (XSS).
-->
Locate points where user-controlled data is rendered in HTML/JS. Suggest output encoding, sanitization, or CSP headers, and provide updated code examples.

<!--
  RULE: path-traversal
  Mitiga Path Traversal.
-->
Inspect file system access for potential path traversal. Implement safe path resolution, whitelist allowed paths, and sanitize or reject malicious inputs.

<!--
  RULE: command-injection
  Previene Command Injection.
-->
Check for any shell/system calls built from user input. Refactor to use safe APIs or properly escape/validate arguments, avoiding direct system invocations.

<!--
  RULE: ssrf-protection
  Detecta y mitiga Server-Side Request Forgery (SSRF).
-->
Analyze code for server-side HTTP requests constructed from user input. Implement allowlists of target hosts, validate URLs, and use safe HTTP client configurations to prevent SSRF :contentReference[oaicite:2]{index=2}:contentReference[oaicite:3]{index=3}.

<!--
  RULE: csrf-protection
  Evita Cross-Site Request Forgery (CSRF).
-->
Locate endpoints vulnerable to CSRF. Enforce CSRF tokens on state-changing requests or use same-site cookies/double-submit cookies patterns as per OWASP CSRF Prevention Cheat Sheet :contentReference[oaicite:4]{index=4}:contentReference[oaicite:5]{index=5}.

<!--
  RULE: cors-misconfiguration
  Previene vulnerabilidades de CORS.
-->
Inspect CORS configurations for overly permissive settings (e.g., Access-Control-Allow-Origin: *). Enforce strict origin whitelists, validate preflight requests, and avoid echoing untrusted origins :contentReference[oaicite:6]{index=6}:contentReference[oaicite:7]{index=7}.

<!--
  RULE: defense-in-depth
  Genera funcionalidades con defensa en profundidad.
-->
Please create [functionality] that prioritizes security over convenience. Implement layering of controls including input validation, output encoding, authentication checks, rate limiting, logging/auditing, and error handling. After code generation, list remaining potential weaknesses.

<!--
  RULE: zero-trust-principles
  Aplica Zero-Trust y least privilege.
-->
Generate [functionality] with explicit OWASP Top 10 protections. Include robust input validation, strict access control (least privilege), network segmentation, and explain your security reasoning step-by-step.

<!--
  RULE: sensitive-info-exposure
  Evita exposición de información sensible en logs y errores.
-->
Analyze code paths and error handlers for unintended data leakage (stack traces, detailed errors). Refactor to sanitize error messages and configure safe logging levels.
